  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 200 PM CDT Wed Oct 14 2026\n\nClouds, scattered showers, and a few embedded thunderstorms have\nbeen on the increase from S to N today. Most areas won't see\nsunshine again until Friday AM. The initial wave of rain today\nis scattered, but there are some embedded heavier cores with a\nfew rumbles of thunder. Latest guidance lifts this activity S to\nN through the remainder of the day, with perhaps some weakening\nwith northern extent/time.\n\nA much larger, and more persistent, shield of light to moderate\nrain (and again, a few embedded weak thunderstorms) will spread\nSW to NE across the entire region late tonight, and continue\nthrough most, if not all, of the morning hours Thursday. Not\nmuch change to expected rainfall amounts as both the latest HREF\nand EPS are in good agreement. Most areas will receive around \n0.25\" to 0.75\". Very isolated higher amounts up to 1.5\" will be \npossible, but this will be more of the exception than the rule.\nCoverage and intensity will taper off from SW/S to NE/N Thursday\nafternoon. Most areas should be done with rain by around \nsunset Thursday, though a few sprinkles/very light showers could\nlinger along the Hwy 81 corridor a couple hours longer. North\nwinds will be a bit breezy on Thursday as the entire system\nlifts through the Plains and into the Upper Midwest - so even\nwhen it's not raining, its going to feel quite chilly and raw.\nHigh temperatures will only be in the 50s, but it could feel\nlike mid to upper 40s at times with the breeze. Despite clearing\nskies Thursday night, not expecting frost due to breezy\nconditions persisting all night.\n\nStrongest high pressure of the fall season thus far will ooze\ninto the region on Friday. This will gradually lower wind speeds\non Friday, but temperatures will remain seasonably cool only in\nthe 50s. The cool daytime, in conjunction with clear skies, \nweak winds along sfc ridge axis, and recent moisture will likely\nset the stage for widespread (perhaps significant/thick) frost \nFriday night into Saturday morning. Return flow will be on the \nincrease towards dawn over the far W/NW, but damage may already \nbe done in these areas by then. Forecast has been cooling \nslightly with each and every cycle, and now all but far SE zones\nare forecast to dip to, or just below, freezing Sat AM. As \nsuch, will likely by issuing frost/freeze headlines within the \nnext 12-18 hours for this threat.\n\nTemperatures will warm nicely Saturday afternoon in the dry\nautumn airmass...and expect further moderation to temperatures\nSunday into early next week. In fact, upper 60s to upper 70s are\nforecast each day Sunday through Wednesday next week, with our\nKS zones possibly even touching the low 80s late in the period.\nMay have another period of patchy frost to contend with Saturday\nnight, but a steady southerly breeze should prevent another\nround of widespread/thick frost...and lows only warm further\nfrom there next several days. So those looking to extend the\ngrowing season should be able to get at least another week,\nprovided protective measures are taken Fri night-Sat AM.",
